WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.487
Hi! I'm Sandra, from HEARa.

2
00:00:03.487 --> 00:00:07.421
Welcome to Day 6 of our 10 day Lipreading Challenge,

3
00:00:07.421 --> 00:00:09.381
designed to help you develop

4
00:00:09.381 --> 00:00:11.965
and practice your lip reading skills!

5
00:00:11.965 --> 00:00:15.711
Hearing loss can have a profound impact on your life.

6
00:00:15.711 --> 00:00:17.760
It touches every interaction.

7
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.109
It can affect your career,

8
00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:21.453
your relationships,

9
00:00:21.453 --> 00:00:22.946
and your happiness.

10
00:00:23.146 --> 00:00:28.003
Join me to explore tips and tricks on this YouTube channel

11
00:00:28.003 --> 00:00:30.912
to bring confidence and clarity to

12
00:00:30.912 --> 00:00:33.473
relationships in all areas of your life.

13
00:00:33.473 --> 00:00:36.256
Yesterday, we started with vowels.

14
00:00:36.256 --> 00:00:39.345
There's two reasons to learn about vowels:

15
00:00:39.345 --> 00:00:42.360
there's a vowel in every word in English,

16
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.962
and it's mostly the vowels

17
00:00:44.962 --> 00:00:47.640
that give rise to accents in English.

18
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.467
Here's another thing to know

19
00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:52.379
about vowels:

20
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.455
they have low pitch energy.

21
00:00:55.456 --> 00:00:58.113
So when you're yelling or speaking

22
00:00:58.113 --> 00:01:02.065
loudly, it's mostly the vowel sounds

23
00:01:02.065 --> 00:01:03.899
that are getting louder.

24
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.315
Remember vowels have that

25
00:01:06.315 --> 00:01:07.860
open vocal tract

26
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.863
and that allows you to

27
00:01:09.863 --> 00:01:11.520
get that sound energy out.

28
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.437
Versus with a high-pitched consonant,

29
00:01:15.438 --> 00:01:18.514
the sound energy has to go through the

30
00:01:18.514 --> 00:01:20.893
constriction or the closure.

31
00:01:20.893 --> 00:01:25.000
So for example, for the F or the V sound,

32
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.505
the air has to go through

33
00:01:26.505 --> 00:01:31.289
the constriction of the teeth and the lip.

34
00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.321
Try yelling a V.

35
00:01:34.792 --> 00:01:38.624
So if you find that you can hear, but you

36
00:01:38.624 --> 00:01:39.919
can't understand,

37
00:01:39.919 --> 00:01:45.179
it might be because you're mostly hearing the vowel sounds.

38
00:01:45.179 --> 00:01:51.913
Most people with permanent hearing loss have a sloping hearing loss

39
00:01:51.913 --> 00:01:56.360
where the high frequency sounds are most affected.

40
00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:01.016
The low pitch vowels for example, are audible.

41
00:02:01.016 --> 00:02:05.433
But those high pitch consonants, the

42
00:02:05.433 --> 00:02:07.840
ones that can't really be said loudly

43
00:02:07.841 --> 00:02:10.687
because of the closure or constriction,

44
00:02:10.687 --> 00:02:12.879
are the ones that are affected.

45
00:02:13.028 --> 00:02:15.637
So let's look more closely at the long

46
00:02:15.637 --> 00:02:17.846
vowels and the diphthongs

47
00:02:18.005 --> 00:02:20.734
and see how they are formed.

48
00:02:20.833 --> 00:02:22.752
Remember the long vowels

49
00:02:22.753 --> 00:02:24.250
are pronounced the same way they are

50
00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.621
said in the alphabet:

51
00:02:26.621 --> 00:02:29.393
A, E, I, O, and U.

52
00:02:29.393 --> 00:02:36.194
For the vowel A,
the lips draw back as in LATE.

53
00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:38.921
For the vowel E,

54
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.211
the lips come back even more,

55
00:02:42.212 --> 00:02:43.269
and the opening

56
00:02:43.269 --> 00:02:46.948
between the lips is narrower, as in BEEF.

57
00:02:48.602 --> 00:02:53.090
For the vowel I, your mouth opens wide

58
00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.504
like it would if your physician put a

59
00:02:55.603 --> 00:02:57.864
tongue depressor in your mouth

60
00:02:57.865 --> 00:03:00.077
and asked you to say “AH.” And then as

61
00:03:00.077 --> 00:03:01.760
the sound completes,

62
00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.737
the lips come together for the EE.

63
00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.017
AH-EE, as in WIFE.

64
00:03:10.191 --> 00:03:11.644
The O sound is pretty easy.

65
00:03:11.645 --> 00:03:17.394
Your lips form a small circle,
as in THOSE.

66
00:03:18.147 --> 00:03:21.747
For the U sound,
your lips thrust forward 

67
00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.460
and then come close together,
as in FLUTE. 

68
00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:34.680
For the diphthong OI,
the lips come forward 

69
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.680
to form that small circle O,

70
00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.818
and then they come backwards and have a narrow opening

71
00:03:42.818 --> 00:03:44.340
for the EE, BOY.

72
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.024
For the diphthong OW,

73
00:03:48.024 --> 00:03:52.256
also spelled OU, the mouth opens wide

74
00:03:52.256 --> 00:03:57.635
for the AH and then the jaw closes
for the U, AH-OU,

75
00:03:57.635 --> 00:04:00.051
as in the word SHOUT.

76
00:04:00.051 --> 00:04:04.398
The last vowel I'm going to cover is the ER.

77
00:04:04.398 --> 00:04:11.280
The lips round and pucker, as in BERT, or SHIRT.

78
00:04:11.281 --> 00:04:14.094
Let's get some practice lip reading the

79
00:04:14.094 --> 00:04:16.620
long vowels
and diphthongs.

80
